# obstack compose — operator settings
#
# Copy this file to `.env` beside docker-compose.yml and edit it; compose reads
# it whichever way the stack is started, from this directory or from the repo
# root with `-f deploy/compose/docker-compose.yml`. Exporting the same names in
# your shell works too and wins over the file.
#
#   cp deploy/compose/.env.example deploy/compose/.env
#
# Every value below is optional EXCEPT the first one. Leave a line commented out
# to take the default in docker-compose.yml — the defaults are dev credentials
# that live in this repository, which is only defensible because every port this
# bundle publishes is bound to 127.0.0.1. Change them before binding anything to
# a network, and change them from `docker compose down -v`: Postgres reads its
# password at initdb time only, so editing it against an existing volume rotates
# nothing and only breaks the DSNs.

# REQUIRED — the key that signs session cookies. There is deliberately no
# default: a cookie-signing secret shipped inside a distributable bundle is a
# vulnerability every install would share. With this unset the web container
# refuses to start and says so; nothing else in the stack needs it.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
OBSTACK_BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# The origin your browser reaches the app on; it is also the origin the invite
# and verification links in your users' mail are written against. The default is
# http://localhost:3000, the address a laptop install answers on.
#
# An http:// origin downgrades the session cookie away from the `__Secure-` name
# better-auth issues at an https origin (D119) — unavoidable over plain http,
# and the reason to set this to the real https origin the moment the app is
# behind TLS.
#OBSTACK_BETTER_AUTH_URL=https://obstack.example.com

# ClickHouse (telemetry). Two users, one per direction: ingest owns the database
# and its DDL, the web app holds a read-only grant. See README.md, "Users".
#OBSTACK_CLICKHOUSE_INGEST_PASSWORD=
#OBSTACK_CLICKHOUSE_WEB_PASSWORD=

# Postgres (identity, saved views, API keys) — one user, `obstack`, shared by
# ingest and the web app.
#OBSTACK_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# The Vercel log drain's signature secret (optional). obstack drains
# authenticate with an ordinary ingest key in an `Authorization: Bearer` custom
# header, exactly like every other export; this is the separate secret Vercel
# signs the delivery body with, shown beside the drain in its settings. Set it
# and ingest verifies `x-vercel-signature` on every delivery; leave it unset and
# the bearer key is that route's only credential. Either way ingest logs which
# posture it started in — an unset secret is never silent non-verification.
#OBSTACK_VERCEL_DRAIN_SECRET=
